<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bidar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bidar is a city on a plateau in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ahmad Shah I made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> in the 1420s, and it was later the seat of the Barid Shahi rulers of the Bidar Sultanate until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> annexed it in 1619. Its fort, the ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> and the tombs of the Bahmani and Barid Shahi kings survive, and the city gave its name to bidriware metalwork.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bidar.jpg" alt="Bidar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sunnya343 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_Nanak_Jhira_Sahib_gurdwara_in_Bidar,_Jul_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Russian merchant Afanasy Nikitin stayed in <b>Bidar</b> around 1470 and wrote one of the earliest European descriptions of the Bahmani court and its people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bijapur Sultanate was one of five states that emerged from the break-up of the Bahmani Sultanate, which had ruled the Deccan from Gulbarga and later <b>Bidar</b> since 1347.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive defeat came on 23 January 1565 at the Battle of Talikota, when the combined armies of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda and <b>Bidar</b> destroyed the Vijayanagara army and killed its leader Aliya Rama Raya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tailapa ruled first from Manyakheta, and the dynasty later moved its seat to Kalyani, modern Basavakalyan in <b>Bidar</b> district, under Someshvara I. Over two centuries his successors made the Deccan the centre of a long rivalry with the Chola Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet the same sultan, a Sanskrit inscription records, repaired a temple of Shiva and Parvati at <b>Bidar</b> after his conquest of the Deccan.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Qasim Barid, a Turkic noble, became chief minister of the declining Bahmani Sultanate in the 1490s and controlled its last sultans from <b>Bidar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bidar-sultanate">Bidar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
